{
  "step_1": "The decision reason focuses on the least probable outcomes of each lottery, defined as the most favorable outcome with the lowest probability. For Lottery A, the outcomes are -4000 Euros (80%) and 0 Euros (20%). The most favorable outcome is 0 Euros, which occurs with 20% probability—the minimum probability among the outcomes of A. For Lottery B, the only outcome is -3000 Euros with 100% probability; thus, it has no outcome with a lower probability than 100%, and its least probable (and only) outcome is -3000 Euros. The decision reason compares these least probable outcomes: 0 Euros (from A) vs. -3000 Euros (from B). Since 0 Euros is more favorable than -3000 Euros, the reason would prefer Lottery A. Therefore, the decision reason can be applied to this decision problem, and it would recommend choosing Lottery A.",
  "step_2": "The individual reports choosing Lottery A because having an 80% chance of losing 4000 Euros 'sounds better' than a certain loss of 3000 Euros. The key justification is the 20% chance of ending up with 0 Euros in Lottery A, which represents a chance to avoid any loss. In contrast, the individual notes that Lottery B guarantees a loss of 3000 Euros with 100% certainty. The individual explicitly highlights the possibility of escaping loss in A due to the 20% probability outcome of 0 Euros. The deliberation centers on the existence and desirability of this non-loss outcome, even though it is less likely. The focus is on the favorable nature of the 0 Euro outcome and its non-zero probability, contrasting it with the certainty of loss in B.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the least probable outcome that is most favorable—i.e., the best outcome with the minimum probability. In Lottery A, that is 0 Euros with 20% probability; in Lottery B, it is -3000 Euros with 100% probability. The reason prefers A because 0 > -3000. The individual’s verbal report explicitly emphasizes the 20% chance of getting 0 Euros as a key factor, describing it as a valuable chance to avoid loss, and contrasts it with the certainty of loss in B. This aligns directly with focusing on the least probable (i.e., 20% vs. effectively no uncertainty in B) and most favorable outcome. Although the term 'least probable' might intuitively seem to refer to the rarest outcome, in B there is no variation in probability, so the only outcome is trivially the least probable. The individual’s attention to the 20% chance of a better outcome and its favorable nature matches the core logic of the decision reason. Therefore, both the relevant information (the best low-probability outcome) and the justification (preferring A due to the chance of avoiding loss) align with the decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 100
